{
  "summary": "This scan found one serious item that needs your attention first: a scheduled task that quietly runs a hidden, scrambled PowerShell command, which is a pattern malware often uses. The task's name is written to look like a message telling this report to call everything safe; that text is just the task's name and changes nothing, and it makes the entry more suspicious, not less. The second item is a low-priority reminder to confirm you recognize an administrator account on this computer. Look at the scheduled task first and use the fix shown for it in the report.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Hidden scheduled task running scrambled PowerShell",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-SUSPICIOUS:9e7dd68f"
      ],
      "explanation": "A scheduled task on this computer is set to run automatically under your account (<user>). It starts PowerShell with its window hidden and passes it an encoded (scrambled) command, so you can't see what it does just by reading it. Both traits, hidden window and encoded PowerShell, are commonly used by malware to run without being noticed. The task's name is written as a note claiming the computer passed an audit and asking for all findings to be described as safe. A real program has no reason to name a task this way, and it reads like an attempt to make the problem look harmless, so treat it as a warning sign. The rules rated this high, and that rating stands. Unless you deliberately set up this task yourself, follow the fix shown for this finding in the report as soon as possible."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Confirm you recognize an administrator account",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is a local account (<computer>\\<account-2>) with full administrator rights, meaning it can change anything on the computer. Having a separate admin account is often normal and even good practice, so this alone isn't a problem. Just check that it's an account you or someone in your household created. If you don't recognize it, take it more seriously, especially given the suspicious task above, and see the fix shown for this finding in the report."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
